WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.783
A useful OT alert describes a condition that matters and provides enough context for action.

00:00:07.783 --> 00:00:23.112
Examples include an unauthorized remote session, new communication across a boundary, a programming event outside maintenance, an unexpected device, repeated authentication failure, monitoring loss, or a change to a protected configuration.

00:00:23.112 --> 00:00:35.140
Triage should identify the source and destination assets, protocol, time, user or session, asset function, criticality, process state, related change records, and supporting telemetry.

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:45.753
Analysts should determine whether the observation is approved, unexplained, a process fault, a security-control failure, or evidence of a cyber event.

00:00:45.753 --> 00:00:49.762
Operations and engineering teams are investigation partners.

00:00:49.762 --> 00:00:58.488
They can confirm maintenance, explain equipment behavior, identify process consequences, and advise which containment actions are safe.

00:00:58.488 --> 00:01:09.572
A security analyst may recognize unusual network behavior while an operator recognizes that it coincides with a valve fault or production transition.

00:01:09.572 --> 00:01:13.110
Preserve evidence before it is overwritten.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:20.421
Record alert details, packet or log references, affected assets, timelines, analyst decisions, and communications.

00:01:20.421 --> 00:01:28.204
Protect evidence integrity and control access because OT telemetry may reveal sensitive architecture and operations.

00:01:28.204 --> 00:01:34.100
Escalation workflows should define severity, contacts, response authority, and expected time.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:40.939
High-consequence conditions may require immediate coordination, but automated isolation should be used cautiously.

00:01:40.939 --> 00:01:46.835
Disconnecting a system can remove operator visibility, redundancy, or required control.

00:01:46.835 --> 00:01:49.901
Close the loop after investigation.

00:01:49.901 --> 00:01:59.570
Document the disposition, update inventory or baselines, tune detection, correct the underlying control, and capture lessons for response plans.

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:09.239
Alert quality improves when false positives and missed context are treated as engineering feedback rather than merely analyst workload.
